  "query_preview": "..The upper-pattern is currently defined by a strong\nupper-low centered across northeast California/northwest Nevada\nand a shortwave ridge across southeast Arizona and southwest \nNew Mexico. Strong NLY flow along the western periphery of the\nupper-low will allow it to dig south today into central \nCalifornia with a vort lobe rotating into southern California \nalong the base of the trough itself by 28/00Z this afternoon. \n\nThis system is anomalously strong, and will serve to tighter \npressure gradients into Arizona, with breezy to windy conditions\nspreading across the western half of the state. Expect SWLY \nwinds 15 to 25 mph with gusts to 35 mph from Tucson west, with \nlighter SLY/SWLY winds extending into the eastern portions of \nthe CWA. The strongest winds should extend across the Tohono \nO'odham Nation and western Santa Cruz county. I wouldn't \nanticipate these winds to produce significant blowing dust \nconcerns across the area, but I would be surprised to see \npatchy blowing dust reducing visibility between 1-3 miles across\nportions of the TO Nation.\n\nIn addition to the winds, this system will bring with it cooler\ntemperatures extending as far east as southeast Arizona. Western\nPima county will see afternoon readings in the mid 80s the next\nfew days, which is approximately 10-14 degrees below normal for\nthis time of year. Cooling will be more subtle the farther east\nyou go (away from the upper-low) with the Tucson Metro Area\nexperiencing afternoon high temperatures in the lower 90s, which\nis approximately 3 to 6 degrees below normal.\n\nCooler temperatures will keep minimum RHs above critical levels\n(15 to 30 percent) across western fire weather zone 150, but \nconditions will be elevated/near-critical in the eastern portions\nof fire weather zone 150 and into zone 151. Although conditions\nmay briefly approach critical levels, they should be short lived\nand not widespread enough to warrant a fire weather headline.\nThis is supported by the SPC which has the aforementioned locations\nin an \"Elevated\" category with their Fire Weather Outlook for \ntoday and Thursday.\n\nOtherwise, the ensemble members of the GEFS and ECMWF are still\nindicating a strong ridge of high pressure building into the \nDesert Southwest/Four Corners region the middle of next week.\nThis pattern would usher in deeper moisture into our neck of the\nwoods from the southeast/east. The 27/03Z NBM probability of\n55+ degree dewpoints at KTUS are 10-20 percent next Wednesday \nthrough next Friday. This moisture would be sufficient for a \nchance of thunderstorms to the eastern half of Southeast \nArizona...but not moist enough for any significant rainfall.\nThis pattern heavily leans toward gusty thunderstorm outflows/\nblowing dust.",
